<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-4-arc-length" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Arclength</title>

  <p><alert>Increments of length.</alert> In this section,
  we give an integral formula to compute the length of a curve,
  by the same Method of Slice Analysis we used in <m>\S5.2</m> to compute volume,
  and in <m>\S5.3</m> to compute work (see end <m>\S5.2</m>).</p>

  <p>We want the arclength <m>L</m> of a graph curve <m>y=f(x)</m> for <m>x\in[a,b]</m>.
  We cut the curve into <m>n</m> bits determined by <m>\Delta x</m>-increments of <m>x\in[a,b]</m>.
  (In the picture, <m>n=5</m>.)</p>

  <figure xml:id="fig-8-1-length-placeholder">
    <caption>Placeholder for 8-1-Length.</caption>
    <image width="60%">
      <shortdescription>Boilerplate TikZ placeholder for 8-1-Length.png.</shortdescription>
      <latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}
  \begin{scope}[x=1.35cm,y=1.05cm]
    \def\a{0.5}
    \def\b{4.0}

    \draw[->] (0,0) -- (4.55,0) node[right] {$x$};
    \draw[->] (0,0) -- (0,3.05) node[above] {$y$};

    \draw[thick,domain=0.35:4.25,samples=180,smooth]
      plot (\x,{0.3+0.45*\x+0.08*\x*\x});
    \node[above right] at (2.6,2.2) {$y=f(x)$};

    \foreach \k in {0,1,2,3,4,5} {
      \pgfmathsetmacro\xx{0.5+0.7*\k}
      \pgfmathsetmacro\yy{0.3+0.45*\xx+0.08*\xx*\xx}
      \fill (\xx,\yy) circle (1.05pt);
      \draw[densely dotted] (\xx,0) -- (\xx,\yy);
    }

    \draw[orange!75!black,thick]
      (0.5,{0.3+0.45*0.5+0.08*0.5*0.5}) --
      (1.2,{0.3+0.45*1.2+0.08*1.2*1.2}) --
      (1.9,{0.3+0.45*1.9+0.08*1.9*1.9}) --
      (2.6,{0.3+0.45*2.6+0.08*2.6*2.6}) --
      (3.3,{0.3+0.45*3.3+0.08*3.3*3.3}) --
      (4.0,{0.3+0.45*4.0+0.08*4.0*4.0});

    \node[below] at (0.5,0) {$a=x_0$};
    \node[below] at (1.9,0) {$x_i$};
    \node[below] at (2.6,0) {$x_{i+1}$};
    \node[below] at (4.0,0) {$b=x_n$};

    \pgfmathsetmacro\xi{1.9}
    \pgfmathsetmacro\xj{2.6}
    \pgfmathsetmacro\yi{0.3+0.45*\xi+0.08*\xi*\xi}
    \pgfmathsetmacro\yj{0.3+0.45*\xj+0.08*\xj*\xj}

    \draw[blue!70!black,thick] (\xi,\yi) -- (\xj,\yj);
    \draw[dashed] (\xi,\yi) -- (\xj,\yi);
    \draw[dashed] (\xj,\yi) -- (\xj,\yj);

    \draw[<->] (\xi,-0.2) -- (\xj,-0.2);
    \node[below] at ({0.5*(\xi+\xj)},-0.2) {$\Delta x$};

    \draw[<->] (\xj+0.12,\yi) -- (\xj+0.12,\yj);
    \node[right] at (\xj+0.12,{0.5*(\yi+\yj)}) {$\Delta y$};

    \node[above left] at ({0.5*(\xi+\xj)},{0.5*(\yi+\yj)}) {$\Delta L_i$};
  \end{scope}
\end{tikzpicture}
      ]]></latex-image>
    </image>
  </figure>

  <p>Because the bit at the sample point <m>x_i</m> is so short,
  it is well approximated by a straight segment, and we can
  use the Pythagorean Theorem to compute its length:</p>

  <p><md><![CDATA[
\Delta L_i  \ \approx\ \sqrt{(\Delta x)^2+(\Delta y)^2}\,.
  ]]></md></p>

  <p>We want to write this as a term in a Riemann sum,
  so we must write it in the form <m>g(x_i)\,\Delta x</m> for some function <m>g(x)</m>.
  We simply factor out <m>\Delta x</m>:</p>

  <p><md><![CDATA[
\Delta L_i
\ \approx\
\sqrt{\left(1+\tfrac{(\Delta y)^2}{(\Delta x)^2}\right)(\Delta x)^2}
\ =\
\sqrt{1+(\tfrac{\Delta y}{\Delta x})^2}\  \Delta x\,.
  ]]></md></p>

  <p>In the limit as <m>n\to \infty</m>, we get <m>\Delta x\to0</m> and <m>\frac{\Delta y}{\Delta x}\to \frac{dy}{dx} = f'(x_i)</m>, and the Riemann sum total of the <m>\Delta L_i</m>'s becomes an integral:</p>

  <p><md><![CDATA[
L \ =\ \lim_{n\to\infty} \sum_{i=1}^n\Delta L_i \ =\ \lim_{n\to\infty}\sum_{i=1}^n\! \sqrt{1+(\tfrac{\Delta y}{\Delta x})^2}\ \Delta x
\ = \ \int_a^b\!\!\! \sqrt{1+(\tfrac{dy}{dx})^2}\ dx\,.
  ]]></md></p>

  <p>In Newton notation:</p>

  <p><md><![CDATA[
L  \ = \ \int_a^b\!\!\! \sqrt{1+y'(x)^2}\ dx\,.
  ]]></md></p>

  <p><em>example:</em> Compute the arclength of the curve <m>y=x\sqrt x</m> over the
  interval <m>x\in[0,4]</m>. We have <m>\frac{dy}{dx} = (x^{3/2})' = \frac32x^{1/2}</m>,
  so:</p>

  <p><md><![CDATA[
L
\ =\
\int_0^4 \sqrt{1+(\tfrac{dy}{dx})^2\,}\,dx
\ =\
\int_0^4 \sqrt{1+\tfrac94x\,}\,dx
\ =\
\left.\tfrac{8}{27} (1{+}\tfrac94x)^{3/2}\right|_{x=0}^{x=4}
\ =\
\tfrac{8}{27}(\sqrt{10}{-}1)
\ \approx\ 9.07
  ]]></md></p>

  <p>To check this, we compare with the straight-line distance between the endpoints
  <m>(0,0)</m> and <m>(4, 8)</m>: this is <m>\sqrt{4^2+8^2} \approx 8.9</m>, and indeed the
  length of the curve is slightly larger.</p>

  <p><em>example:</em> Compute the circumference of the unit circle,
  which is twice the arclength of the graph <m>y=\sqrt{1{-}x^2\,}</m> for <m>x\in[-1,1]</m>:</p>

  <p><md><![CDATA[
C  \ =\ 2L
\ = \
2\int_{-1}^1\!\! \sqrt{1+\left(\smash{\tfrac{d}{dx}\sqrt{1{-}x^2\,}}\right)^2}\ dx
\ = \
2\int_{-1}^1\! \sqrt{1+\left(\! \tfrac{-2x} {2\sqrt{1{-}x^2\,} }\!\right)^{\!\!2}}\ dx
  ]]></md></p>

  <p><md><![CDATA[
\ = \
2\int_{-1}^1\! \sqrt{ \frac{1-x^2+x^2}{1-x^2} \,}\ dx
\ = \
2\int_{-1}^1\! \frac{1}{ \sqrt{1{-}x^2\,} }\ dx
\ =\
\left.\vphantom{\sum}2\arcsin(x)\,\right|_{x=-1}^{x=1}
\ =\
2\pi\,.
  ]]></md></p>

  <p><em>example:</em> Compute the arclength of the parabola <m>y=x^2</m> over any
  interval <m>x\in[0,b]</m>.</p>

  <p><md><![CDATA[
L
\ =\
\int_0^b\!\!\! {\ts \sqrt{1+(\tfrac{d}{dx}(x^2))^2}}\ dx
\ =\
\int_0^b\!\!\! {\ts \sqrt{1+4x^2\,}}\ dx\,.
  ]]></md></p>

  <p>To find the indefinite integral, we use the reverse trig substitution (<m>\S7.3</m>):
  <m>x = \frac12\tan(\theta)</m>, <m>\sqrt{1+4x^2\,} = \sec(\theta)</m>, <m>dx = \frac12\sec^2(\theta)\,dx</m>:</p>

  <p><md><![CDATA[
\int\!\! {\ts \sqrt{1+4x^2\,}}\ dx
\ =\
\int\! \tfrac12\sec^3(\theta)\,d\theta
\ =\
\tfrac14
\ln\!\left|\vphantom{A^2}\!\tan(\theta){+}\sec(\theta)\right|
\,+\,\tfrac14\tan(\theta)\sec(\theta),
  ]]></md></p>

  <p>where we use <m>\int\!\sec^3(\theta)\,d\theta</m> from <m>\S7.2</m>.
  Restoring the original variable, <m>\tan(\theta)=2x</m>, <m>\sec(\theta)=\sqrt{1{+}4x^2\,}</m>,
  and taking the definite integral:</p>

  <p><md><![CDATA[
L
\ =\
\left[
\tfrac14\ln\!\left|\vphantom{A^2} 2x{+}\sqrt{1{+}4x^2\,}\,\right|
\,+\,\tfrac12x\sqrt{1{+}4x^2\,}\,
\right]_{x=0}^{x=b}
\ =\
\tfrac14\ln\!\left|\vphantom{A^2} 2b{+}\sqrt{1{+}4b^2\,}\,\right|
\,+\,\tfrac12b\sqrt{1{+}4b^2\,}.
  ]]></md></p>

  <p>Arclength tends to get quite complicated even for quite simple curves!</p>

  <p><em>example:</em> Compute the arclength of the curve <m>y=x^3</m> over <m>x\in[0,1]</m>.</p>

  <p><md><![CDATA[
L
\ =\
\int_0^1\!\!\! {\ts \sqrt{1+(\tfrac{d}{dx}(x^3))^2}}\ dx
\ =\
\int_0^1\!\!\! {\ts \sqrt{1+9x^4\,}}\ dx\,.
  ]]></md></p>

  <p>This is already complicated enough that it has no algebraic antiderivative.<fn>
  The integral can be expressed in terms of an <q>elliptic function</q>, but this is circular reasoning
  since elliptic functions themselves are defined as integrals!
  </fn></p>

  <p>Does this mean the arclength formula is useless? Not at all! We cannot get an answer
  on the algebraic level, but we can still get a numerical answer as accurate as we like.
  This means going from the integral formula for <m>L</m> back to the Riemann sums from which we deduced the integral. For example, taking <m>n=1000</m>, the increment is <m>\Delta x=\frac1{1000}=0.001</m>, and the sample points are <m>x_i=i\,\Delta x = (0.001)i</m>.
  The computer gives:</p>

  <p><md><![CDATA[
L
\ \approx\
\sum_{i=1}^{n} \sqrt{1+9x_i^4\,}\, \Delta x
\ =\
\sum_{i=1}^{1000} \sqrt{1+9(10^{-12})i^4\,}\, (0.001)
\ \approx\ 1.548\,,
  ]]></md></p>

  <p>To gauge the accuracy of this, we re-do it with <m>n=10,000</m>, getting <m>L \approx 1.547</m>,
  so we can be confident that <m>L \approx 1.54</m> is accurate to 2 decimal places.</p>

</section>